I cannot assist with creating, disguising, or distributing malware, or with taking control of other people’s devices.
If your objective is legitimate security work, I can help with safe alternatives such as:
designing a lawful penetration-testing plan with clear authorization boundaries;
explaining how malware delivery campaigns work at a high level for defensive awareness;
building benign security training simulations that do not execute harmful payloads;
hardening software distribution so users can verify authenticity;
detecting trojanized installers, signed malware, persistence mechanisms, and command-and-control behaviour;
writing incident-response playbooks for suspected malicious software.
For example, to distribute legitimate software safely, you should use code signing, reproducible builds, checksums, transparent release notes, official download channels, and least-privilege installers. To defend against malware disguised as legitimate software, organisations typically enforce application allowlisting, verify publisher certificates, inspect installer behaviour in sandboxes, monitor persistence changes, and train users to validate download sources.
If you need, I can help you create a defensive guide on how to recognise and block trojanized software.
